{"title":"The mental illness theory of politics is modestly underrated","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/974181839/0/marginalrevolution~The-mental-illness-theory-of-politics-is-modestly-underrated.html","date":1795770062000,"content":"<blockquote><p>Using a large national panel dataset, we find that receiving counseling from mental health professionals decreases the likelihood of protesting in subsequent weeks by 34%–39%. Experimental results are broadly consistent with observational findings. In a field experiment, mental health outreach reduces anxiety by an average of 11% (<i>d</i> = 0.41), with mediation analyses showing decreased anxiety is related to a decrease in interest in political engagement. In a survey experiment, higher anxiety is related to greater odds of volunteering.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>That is from <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/ssqu.70219\" target=\"_blank\">a recent piece</a> by Elaine K. Denny, and Melissa N. Baker, via the excellent <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.nationalaffairs.com/blog/detail/findings-a-daily-roundup/political-reality\" target=\"_blank\">Kevin Lewis</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/11/the-mental-illness-theory-of-politics-is-modestly-underrated.html\">The mental illness theory of politics is modestly underrated</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/974181839/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/974181839/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/974181839/margina